{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-131014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of these listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-65077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation $4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-131016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x$\n- $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}$ $$\n- $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}=x{e}^{x}({e}^{x})$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.  The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-131019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-6x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}$\n- $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}={e}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={e}^{6x}$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-5}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={x}^{-4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=6.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{e}^{-3x}-{e}^{3x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-65113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-3x}-{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{6x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{3x}+2{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y={x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2{e}^{-3x}-{e}^{3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{5t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}(t)-8{y}^{′}(t)+15y(t)=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{5t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}={e}^{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{3t}={e}^{2+3t}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-79797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{3t}+5{e}^{t}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{8t}"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{t}-{e}^{5t}"] ["math" "y=8{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "y={e}^{2+3t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-79797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{2+3t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-65104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "y=2{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "y=5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "y={x}^{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "y={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{3}=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}}{x}"] ["math" "y=x{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-131032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3393391076fea76f8c79cef9647b68f7e013529113d547524bda34e394f52acd"} {"question" "q-65110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "q-65110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/state.json" "file_sha256" "dba49a9ed88281306b99fdf2561b6f2533dc279e8b63db9fa75cbc1c3292b099"} {"question" "e-6865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/example-6865.json" "file_sha256" "2ed3a9b5e3b891cdf9cd6189378573a9f5722340a955be06e80d16c5ee4757e3"} {"question" "e-6865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then $y(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) =  e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/example-6865.json" "file_sha256" "2ed3a9b5e3b891cdf9cd6189378573a9f5722340a955be06e80d16c5ee4757e3"} {"question" "e-6866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/example-6866.json" "file_sha256" "a38be365b90651bded3f2e5d6063022f872f7f3e1da4b33508600dc568bf632c"} {"question" "e-6866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x) = c y_1(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/example-6866.json" "file_sha256" "a38be365b90651bded3f2e5d6063022f872f7f3e1da4b33508600dc568bf632c"} {"question" "e-6578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/example-6578.json" "file_sha256" "4444b7e1ca9f2cacf43f6ea50a2fef60fa7feef23c5524a2d96d3a4c0ce79f90"} {"question" "e-6578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062820/example-6578.json" "file_sha256" "4444b7e1ca9f2cacf43f6ea50a2fef60fa7feef23c5524a2d96d3a4c0ce79f90"}] "usage" {"e-6866" [] "q-65077" [] "e-6865" [] "e-6578" []} "basis" 2013 "retractions" [[17592186152555 :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(x) = e^{x}$ and $y_{2}(x) = e^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation $y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = 0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = e^{x} + e^{2x}$\n2. $y(x) = e^{x} \\cdot e^{2x}$\n3. $y(x) = \\frac{e^{x}}{e^{2x}}$"] [17592186151923 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n2. $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n3. $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$"] [17592186151923 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to 